    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1030">

                <pb facs="0983" n="725" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg725"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 851.731/27</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in France</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Sharp</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-09-15">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 15, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1098. American concerns complain that telegraph companies refuse to inform them
                    whether cablegrams to Italy are stopped or delivered. Please bring urgently
                    attention Foreign Office with request that arrangements be made whereby cable
                    companies will be permitted to notify senders of cablegrams exchanged between
                    United States and Italy regarding delivery or non-delivery of their messages
                    when such information is asked for. Refer to assurances of French Foreign Office
                    conveyed your 487, January 23.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1030-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
